"""Final coordinated graph proposals, limited to frozen B/C candidates."""
from author import *
def vary(i,mis,third,why,feedback=None):
 k=K[i];old=G[i];other='ABCD'.index(old['graph_test']['economically_plausible_without_graph'][1])
 g(i,O[i]['q'],O[i]['options'][k],O[i]['options'][other],mis,third,feedback or O[i]['feedback'],why,old['graph_evidence_required'],old['numerical_reasoning'])

vary('42028','A $6 tax collected as a fixed fee from each firm; it creates the same marginal incentive as charging $6 on each garment.','A $15 per-garment tax; producers should pay the full social marginal cost in addition to their private marginal cost.','Distinguishes a marginal tax from a fixed charge and the external-cost gap from total social marginal cost.','At the efficient 160-million-garment quantity, MSC is $15 and MPC $9. A $6 per-unit tax aligns the producer’s marginal incentive with social cost. A fixed fee does not change marginal cost, while a $15 tax would count private cost twice.')
vary('42038','A $4 tax makes private and social benefits equal by raising consumers’ underlying willingness to pay by $4.','A $12 tax; the full marginal private benefit is the external harm.','Distinguishes changing net incentives from changing underlying preferences, and marginal harm from the full private benefit.','At the efficient 140-thousand-vape quantity, MPB is $12 and MSB $8. A $4 tax reduces the private net incentive to consume by the external harm; it does not raise underlying willingness to pay.')
vary('42048','A $5 subsidy guarantees a $5 net social gain from every garden, including those already produced.','A $15 subsidy per garden; government must reimburse the whole private marginal cost.','Distinguishes an incentive-correcting marginal subsidy from a guaranteed net gain and from full cost reimbursement.','At 150 gardens, MPC is $15 and MSC $10. The $5 external benefit justifies a matching subsidy. The subsidy itself is a transfer, not a guaranteed $5 net social gain on every existing garden.')
vary('42058','A $2 rider subsidy makes the subsidy payment itself the extra social benefit of each ride.','A $6 rider subsidy; the entire marginal social benefit must be added to the private incentive.','Distinguishes the external benefit from a fiscal transfer and the full social-benefit level.','At 200 thousand rides, MSB is $6 and MPB $4. A $2 subsidy closes the incentive gap. The external benefit exists independently of the subsidy payment, which is a transfer.')
g('42204','The vertical guide marks one quantity of radio programming received by both groups. How should that quantity be treated when adding their demands?','Both receive 40 hours; add their marginal benefits at that shared quantity.','Both receive 30 hours; add their marginal benefits at that shared quantity.','Each receives 40 hours, so add the quantities to obtain 80 hours.','The shared quantity is 40 hours; use only the larger group’s marginal benefit.','The guide marks 40 hours. Radio programming is nonrival: both groups receive those same hours. Add their willingness to pay vertically at 40 hours, not their hours horizontally.','Restores one shared-quantity reading and the vertical-summation concept; removes benefit readings and unit conversions.','Vertical guide at Q=40 hours.','Shared quantity40, not40+40; no monetary conversion.')
vary('42207','A values the next hour at $3,000 and B at $1,000; the relevant combined value is their $2,000 average.','The combined marginal benefit is $4,000; this is also the total benefit of all 40 hours.','Replaces the repeated horizontal-sum matrix with average-versus-sum and marginal-versus-total errors.','At 40 hours, A values the next hour at $3,000 and B at $1,000. Nonrival consumption calls for their sum, $4,000, not the $2,000 average. This is marginal benefit, not the benefit of all hours.')
vary('42352','A 4,000-worker surplus, but this is the number employed after wages finish adjusting.','An 8,000-worker surplus, because all workers willing to work at $20 are surplus workers.','Distinguishes a temporary excess-supply gap from equilibrium employment and from the supply level.','At $20, new supply is 8,000 and demand is 4,000, so excess supply is 4,000. This creates downward wage pressure; the gap is not employment after adjustment.')
vary('42360','A 4,000-worker shortage, which means 4,000 workers are unemployed at this wage.','No shortage, because the plotted quantity of labor supplied at $20 is zero.','Distinguishes unfilled labor demand from unemployment and a zero supplied level from a zero market imbalance.','At $20, demand is 4,000 and new supply is zero. The 4,000-worker shortage is unmet demand for labor, putting upward pressure on wages; it is not unemployment.')
vary('42361','Supply changes from 4,000 to 0 workers; this measures the final decline in employment after wages adjust.','Construction supply shifts left, so the graph proves total employment across all industries falls by 4,000.','Distinguishes a fixed-wage supply comparison from equilibrium employment and from economy-wide effects.','At $20, SL0 supplies 4,000 workers and SL1 supplies zero. Better alternatives reduce construction labor supply at any given wage. This fixed-wage gap is not the eventual employment change and does not measure employment in other industries.')
vary('42363','Supply changes from 4,000 to 0 workers; the demand curve must also shift left by 4,000 at every wage.','Supply is lower at $20, so the new curve shows less willingness to work caused solely by a lower construction wage.','Separates a change in availability at a fixed wage from an inferred demand shift and the student’s wage-only explanation.')
g('42376','The monopsonist has chosen to hire 4,000 nurses. In the graph, what hourly wage does it pay, and which curve determines that wage?','$20, read from labor supply.','$25, read from labor supply.','$20, read from marginal factor cost.','$30, the marginal cost of hiring the last nurse.','At 4,000 nurses (40 hundreds), labor supply shows $20 per hour. The $30 MFC includes the extra wage expense associated with expanding employment; it is not the wage paid.','Restores the supplied employment level and asks for one wage reading plus the supply-versus-MFC distinction.','Labor supply at L=40 hundreds is $20; MFC there is $30.','40 hundreds=4000; wage20.')
g('42587','Compare the left and right portions of IC2 in the graph. What does its changing slope imply about the consumer’s willingness to give up Y for another unit of X?','It becomes flatter to the right, so the consumer gives up progressively less Y for another X.','It becomes steeper to the right, so the consumer gives up progressively more Y for another X.','It becomes flatter to the right, so the consumer’s total utility falls as X increases along IC2.','Its slope changes, so the consumer’s preference ranking must change while moving along IC2.','IC2 becomes flatter as X rises. Its absolute slope is the marginal rate of substitution, so the consumer is willing to sacrifice less Y for another X. Utility stays constant along the curve.','Restores visual slope interpretation and diminishing MRS without computing two average rates.','IC2 is visibly steeper on its left portion and flatter on its right.','No interval-slope arithmetic; compare local steepness.')
g('42588','At X = 10, which indifference curve passes between Y = 5 and Y = 10, and what can its label tell us?','IC2; the label identifies a preference rank, not an amount of satisfaction.','IC3; the label identifies a preference rank, not an amount of satisfaction.','IC2; its number means twice the satisfaction represented by IC1.','IC1; its number is the dollar cost of every bundle on it.','At X = 10, IC2 is near Y = 9. Curve labels identify ordinal ranks; their numerical ratios do not measure satisfaction.','Uses one curve identification and the original ordinal-versus-cardinal distinction instead of two coordinate bands.','At X10, IC2 lies between Y5 and10.','One coordinate-band reading; no utility-ratio calculation.')
g('P62B-ELAS-E-028','Which demand curve is steeper in the graph, and why is that visual slope different from price elasticity of demand?','D2 is steeper; slope uses the axis units, whereas elasticity compares percentage changes.','D1 is steeper; slope uses the axis units, whereas elasticity compares percentage changes.','D2 is steeper; its numerical slope is also its price elasticity at every point.','D1 is steeper; changing dollars to cents leaves the numerical slope unchanged.','D2 is the steeper curve. A slope depends on the units on the axes. Elasticity uses percentage changes, so it is not the numerical slope and requires no unit-specific scale.','Restores the units-versus-percentages concept with one visual comparison, removing two midpoint calculations.','D2 visibly steeper than D1 on ELAS-02.','No midpoint computation required.')
vary('ECON-MG-MEDIUM-186','From 100 to 80 units; the 20 units no longer sold become tax revenue.','From 100 to 80 units; all buyer payments become a social loss rather than a payment to sellers or government.','Replaces duplicated no-wedge claims with quantity-versus-revenue and transfer-versus-loss errors.','The tax lowers sales from 100 to 80. Buyers pay $18 and sellers receive $12, leaving a $6 wedge that prevents mutually beneficial trades. Lost units are not tax revenue; revenue is the tax times units still traded.')
vary('ECON-MG-MEDIUM-189','From 100 to 80 units; the buyer’s $3 price increase is the entire wedge because the seller’s loss does not count.','From 80 to 100 units; using the untaxed intersection as the after-tax quantity measures the sales response.','Adds partial-incidence and wrong-baseline errors instead of repeating the same no-wedge misconception.','The original quantity is 100 and the taxed quantity 80. The wedge is $18 − $12 = $6, including the $3 rise in buyer price and the $3 fall in seller receipt from the original $15.')
vary('P62B-ELAS-EL-038','10 and 40 units; those increases are also the new total quantities supplied.','70 and 100 units; subtracting the common starting quantity is unnecessary when measuring the increases.','Distinguishes response changes from final levels and preserves the time-to-adjust interpretation.','Starting from 60 units, the quantities at the higher price are 70 and 100. The increases are 10 and 40; the larger long-run response reflects more adjustment time.')
vary('P62B-ELAS-M-039','10 and 40 units; the larger long-run increase proves every input can adjust fully in the short run.','10 and 100 units; compare the short-run change with the long-run final level to measure responsiveness.','Uses an adjustment-horizon error and a mixed change/level comparison instead of a duplicated reversed-elasticity claim.')
vary('P62C-CPS-L-024','8 units: total surplus is maximized only because the buyer’s payment equals the seller’s receipt.','8 units: the market price is itself the total social gain from those trades.','Distinguishes marginal efficiency from accounting equality and a price level from total surplus.','At E, quantity is 8 and marginal value equals marginal cost at $14. That marginal equality identifies efficiency under the stated assumptions. Equal transfer payments or equal surplus shares do not establish efficiency.')
vary('P62C-CPS-M-029','8 units: this quantity maximizes total surplus only if no producer surplus is earned.','10 units: the price at E can be used as the efficient quantity because demand equals supply there.','Adds distribution-versus-efficiency and axis-confusion errors while retaining an economically plausible alternative quantity.')
vary('P62E-COP-H-019','$240 of total fixed cost; dividing this gap by output is unnecessary when reporting average fixed cost.','$240 of total cost; the TC–TVC gap includes all variable inputs used in production.','Distinguishes total from average fixed cost and the fixed component from full cost.')
vary('P62E-COP-L-053','$240 of average fixed cost; it is the fixed cost per unit at every output.','$240 of total variable cost; these costs remain when output is zero.','Uses total-versus-per-unit and zero-output classification errors at the existing introductory tier.')
vary('P62E-COP-M-024','$240 of total fixed cost; this amount must be added again for each additional unit produced.','$0 of fixed cost, because TC and TVC rise by the same amount when output changes.','Distinguishes the level of a fixed cost from marginal changes and avoids the repeated fixed-versus-marginal matrix.')
vary('P62E-COP-H-018','About $12 per unit; the gap shrinks because each additional unit eliminates $12 of the firm’s total fixed cost.','About $12 at Q = 20 and $42 at Q = 70; fixed cost per unit rises in proportion to output.','Tests the denominator relationship against repeated subtraction and proportional-growth mistakes.','The gap is AFC: $240/20 = $12, and $240/70 ≈ $3.43. Total fixed cost stays $240; the per-unit gap falls because output is the denominator.')
vary('P62E-COP-L-054','About $12 per unit; this is the extra cost of the next unit, so a smaller gap means lower MC.','About $12 per unit; it remains $12 at Q = 70 because total fixed cost is unchanged.','Separates an average fixed cost from marginal cost and distinguishes a constant total from a declining average.')
vary('P62F-PC-EL-038','$1,800; entry lowers market price, but an incumbent’s MR stays at its original level because that firm is a price taker.','$3,600; entry expands supply and lowers price until this sales revenue is zero.','Preserves the advanced market-to-firm adjustment chain while distinguishing price-taking from a fixed price and profit from revenue.','At P = $40, Q = 90 and ATC = $20, profit is $1,800; revenue is $3,600. Entry expands market supply and lowers the price faced by each price-taking firm. Firm MR falls and output contracts along rising MC until P = minimum ATC and economic profit is zero.')
vary('P62F-PC-H-042','$1,800; entry lowers price, but incumbent output must rise because total market supply rises.','$2,250; price minus AVC measures economic profit and predicts entry until P equals AVC.','Separates industry expansion from incumbent contraction and operating contribution from economic profit.','Profit is (40 − 20) × 90 = $1,800, not (40 − 15) × 90 = $2,250. Entry raises market supply, lowers price and reduces each incumbent’s output along MC. Long-run zero profit requires P = ATC, not merely AVC.')
vary('P62F-PC-L-097','$1,800; entry lowers price and incumbent output, which proves total industry output must also fall.','$1,800; free entry ends only when the price–AVC margin is zero, even if ATC remains above price.','Preserves a multi-stage adjustment diagnosis and distinguishes firm versus industry output and short-run versus long-run viability.')
vary('P62F-PC-EL-044','P = $25, Q = 50; zero economic profit means the firm covers explicit costs but cannot cover the owners’ opportunity costs.','P = $25, Q = 50; P = MC alone rules out further entry even when P exceeds ATC.','Distinguishes economic from accounting cost coverage and a necessary marginal rule from long-run sufficiency.')
vary('P62F-PC-M-048','P = $25, Q = 50; normal returns to the owners are excluded from ATC, so economic profit must be positive.','P = $25, Q = 50; P above AVC measures positive economic profit even when P equals ATC.','Varies the cost-definition and AVC-versus-ATC errors while preserving the benchmark interpretation.')
vary('P62G-MON-H-032','36 units; expanding toward demand = MC must raise profit because price remains above MC on the extra units.','60 units; maximizing total surplus also maximizes the monopolist’s profit even when extra sales lower revenue on earlier units.','Distinguishes marginal revenue from price and private profit from total surplus without repeating a two-by-two rule swap.','Profit maximization uses MR = MC at 36 units. Demand crosses MC near 60, the social benchmark. Between them price exceeds MC but MR is below MC, so expansion raises total surplus while reducing monopoly profit.')
vary('P62G-MON-H-036','40 units; the firm should expand to lower ATC even when the extra units cost more than the revenue they add.','About 67 units; the largest output with price covering MC maximizes profit regardless of marginal revenue.','Adds average-cost minimization and price-as-marginal-revenue mistakes while retaining the private-versus-social output distinction.')
vary('P62H-MCMP-H-016','$76; entry eliminates the price–MC markup whenever it eliminates economic profit.','$76; entry shifts each incumbent’s marginal cost down until its existing demand curve becomes tangent to ATC.','Distinguishes profit from markup and demand adjustment from an unsupported cost shift.','At Q = 24, P = ATC = $76 while MC = $49.60. Entry reduces incumbent demand until profit is zero, but markup and output below minimum ATC remain. Entry need not shift the firm’s cost curves.')
vary('P62H-MCMP-EL-023','$82; the $33.60 price–MC gap is the firm’s economic profit per unit after entry.','$82; a higher number of competitors shifts each incumbent’s demand outward until P = ATC.','Separates a surviving markup from profit and incumbent demand from market-wide entry.','At Q = 28, P = ATC = $82 and MC = $48.40. The $33.60 markup is not profit per unit; profit uses P − ATC. Entry shifts incumbent demand inward.')
vary('P62H-MCMP-L-094','$27; price equals ATC after entry, so the firm is productively efficient even though its output remains below minimum-ATC output.','$27; entry ends when P equals AVC, leaving no reason to cover the remaining fixed costs in the long run.','Retains advanced diagnosis of zero profit with excess capacity and distinguishes long-run cost recovery from the shutdown condition.','At Q = 36, P = ATC = $27 and output is below the minimum-ATC quantity of 50. Entry has removed economic profit, not excess capacity. Productive efficiency requires minimum ATC; covering AVC alone is insufficient for long-run survival.')

assert set(P)<=set(S['unique_union'])
assert set(S['set_B'])|set(S['set_C'])<=set(P)
save()
